{"id": "22641", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=22641", "title": "Mogo\u0219oaia", "text": "Mogo\u015foaia is a village in Muntenia.\nUnderstand.\nLocated right outside Bucharest, Mogo\u015foaia is home to an impressive late 17th-early 18th century princely palace.\nGet in.\nBus 460 links Bucharest with Mogo\u015foaia and Buftea; this bus departs from Laromet transport terminal (north-western part of Bucharest) every 15-20 minutes on weekdays and every 45-50 minutes on weekends. To get to the palace get off at the ninth stop. It's also possible to reach Mogo\u015foaia by minibus (the ones heading to Buftea or to Ploie\u015fti via Buftea); they leave several times per hour from an area just east of Gara de Nord train station.\nTrains between the airport and central Bucharest calls here. Journey time is just 10 minutes from Gara de Nord, the main railway station.\nGet around.\nThe palace is a 600 meters walk from the main road where the buses and minibuses stop."}
{"id": "22643", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=22643", "title": "Mogollon Rim", "text": "The Mogollon Rim is a geologic feature that separates the high desert of northeastern Arizona from the low desert of southeastern Arizona. The rim is 1000 ft to 2000 ft high and covered with forest. Fort Apache, San Carlos and Tonto Apache Indian reservations are located on the rim.\nUnderstand.\nFor the purposes of this article, the Mogollon Rim is a region in Eastern Arizona taken to be between north-south roughly between Interstate 40 / Historic U.S. Route 66 of Northern Arizona and Interstate 10 of Southeast Arizona, and east-west between Southwest New Mexico and Greater Phoenix.\nGeography.\nThe Mogollon Rim is an escarpment defining the southwestern edge of the Colorado Plateau, cutting across this region from the northwest to southeast.\nThe Colorado Plateau is largely made up of high desert, with scattered areas of forests. In the southwest corner of the Colorado Plateau lies the Grand Canyon of the Colorado River. Much of the Plateau's landscape is related, in both appearance and geologic history, to the Grand Canyon. The nickname \"Red Rock Country\" suggests the brightly colored rock left bare to the view by dryness and erosion. Domes, hoodoos, fins, reefs, river narrows, natural bridges, and slot canyons are only some of the additional features typical of the Plateau.\nSouth of the Mogollon Rim, much of the land lies from 4,000 to elevation, with the escarpment of the rim rising to about 8,000 ft. Extensive Ponderosa pine forests are found both on the slopes of the Rim and on the plateau north of it. Extensive boundary flora and fauna transition from the low desert to the south, up the escarpment, to the plateau.\nHistory.\nOld West...\nCopper mining...\nGet around.\nDue to the Rim, travel within the region often is not as straight-as-a-crow; even major highways like US 60 must meander around valleys, canyons and rivers.\nA car is essential as there is no public transportation. To access some of the more remote off-road areas, a four-wheel drive is recommended."}
{"id": "22645", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=22645", "title": "Mohale's Hoek", "text": "Mohale's Hoek is a town in southern Lesotho. It is set among the rolling hills and valleys of southern Lesotho.\nUnderstand.\nMohale's Hoek is the capital of the [Mohale's Hoek District. It is an important administrative and commercial centre serving the surrounding rural communities.\nThe area around Mohale's Hoek is largely rural, with many communities depending on agriculture and livestock keeping. The landscape is characterised by hills, open grasslands, valleys and small settlements.\nSesotho is widely spoken, while English is also an official language and is commonly used in education, government and business.\nClimate.\nMohale's Hoek has a temperate highland climate. Summers are generally warm and are the main rainy season, while winters are dry and can be cold, especially during the night and early morning.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nMohale's Hoek is connected by road with Maseru, Mafeteng and other towns in southern Lesotho. Road travel is the main way of reaching the town.\nBy bus.\nBuses, minibuses and shared taxis connect Mohale's Hoek with Maseru and other settlements. Services may vary depending on the route and time of day.\nGet around.\nThe central part of Mohale's Hoek can be explored on foot. Taxis and shared vehicles are available for travelling around the town and to nearby communities.\nEat.\nMohale's Hoek has a number of fine dining options. On the main street (near Nedbank and Fraser's Supermarket) is the delectable Rooster's. Rooster's is known for its fried chicken, pizza and meat pies. Service may be a bit slow but it'll allow you to take in the atmosphere.\nNear Moletsane Road, there is the formidable Mount Maloti Hotel. A \"quick\" lunch menu is available. Sandwiches, shakes, chips, pizza, and beverages galore are available in a cozy setting. You can even dine poolside if you wish! The more pricey buffet is another hotel option.\nJust off the corner of the bus-rank near the Mahe Hut, there is a cute local food shop called Two for Joy. Cheap \"plate\" food is available here for sit-in or take-away. The service is great and so are the Makoena!\nDrink.\nThere are a number of drinking spots around town including the newly built \"Castle Rock\" where you can relax and enjoy Mhoek and the famous Maluti drink.\nSleep.\nOne notable accommodation in the area is the Mount Maloti Hotel."}
{"id": "22646", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=22646", "title": "Mohali", "text": "Mohali, officially Sahibzada Ajit Singh Nagar (SAS Nagar), is a satellite city of Chandigarh and is also an administrative district of the Punjab, state in the Plains of India.\nCope.\nHospitals.\nAdditional hospitals in nearby Chandigarh.\nGo next.\nThe nearest cities to visit are Chandigarh and Panchkula. Kasuali is another nearby location which can be a good uphill spot."}
